    "request": "## Objective\n\nDeliver a production-quality 3D browser first-person shooter for desktop players, using Call of Duty: Warzone as the fidelity reference for its recognizable military visual language, responsive combat feel, HUD, loadout flow, and match loop. Ship original code and original or properly licensed assets; do not use proprietary game files.\n\n## Quality bar\n\n- Research before coding: inspect official Call of Duty materials, representative gameplay footage, screenshots, and interface views. Select at least six high-signal references, record each URL and its purpose in the live progress page, and use them to guide the visual and interaction decisions.\n- Preserve the real web 3D surface: deliver a playable browser game with first-person camera and movement, aiming, shooting, reload/ammo, health or armor, enemy combat, hit feedback, a clear start state, a combat objective, win/lose state, restart flow, and responsive desktop controls. Do not substitute a flat mockup, static scene, or screenshot.\n- Inspect the actual running artifact directly in the browser and compare captures or side-by-side views against the reference bar. Verify the game at the public URL, including the important combat and recovery states.\n- Meet a production-quality bar for visual hierarchy, readable HUD, lighting, feedback, performance, accessibility or operability, error handling, and complete delivery evidence.\n\n## Execution\n\n- Act as the CTO of the game studio and the orchestrator for continuous multi-day work. Own the product and technical vision, roadmap, delegation, quality bar, risks, decisions, integration, and delivery. Keep working ambitiously toward the complete outcome; treat prototypes, demos, and smoke tests as intermediate milestones.\n- Choose the implementation route and divide the project into independently judgeable pieces. Fo
